    "evidence_text": "DESIGN Population based, cross sectional study.   \n\n\nThe association pattern was similar for all headache types.\n\nEvidence was lacking for cognitive impairment for any headache type with or without brain lesions.   \n\n\nMigraine with aura was the only headache type associated with brain infarcts.\n\nOBJECTIVE Chronic physical comorbidity is common in dementia.\n\nOnly hearing impairment made a notable independent contribution to cognitive impairment.   \n\n\nTraditional Chinese acupuncture in tension-type headache: a controlled study.\n\nHowever, the mechanism of migraine complicated with depressive/anxious disorder remains unclear.   \n\n\nOne patient had no scale elevation.\n\nImprovement of headaches and a decrease of several MMPI and STAI 2 scores were observed.\n\nWith time, these patients may develop a depressive disorder.\n\nNeither treatment, however, eliminated headache problems.\n\nBACKGROUND The association between antecedent head injury and AD is inconsistent.   \n\n\nResults were similar for dementia in general.\n\nThe results for mild head injury were inconclusive.\n\nA diffusion tensor magnetic resonance imaging study of brain tissue from patients with migraine.\n\nNo differences were found in FA histogram derived metrics between migraine patients and healthy subjects.\n\nPsychiatric comorbidity in chronic daily headache.\n\n8.7 years).\n\nRESULTS Two hundred sixty-one patients with chronic daily headache were recruited.",
